---
title: Lazy Tools
id: lazy-tools
order: 5
description: "Keep large tool catalogs out of the Code Mode system prompt with lazy tools — the model fetches TypeScript signatures on demand via a discover_tools call."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - code mode
  - lazy tools
  - discover_tools
  - progressive disclosure
  - prompt size
  - tool catalog
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large tool catalogs bloat the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute_typescript"},{"type":"text","value":" system prompt. Every tool you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCodeMode"},{"type":"text","value":" becomes a full TypeScript type stub in that prompt — and at 50+ tools, those stubs can push the effective prompt into the tens of thousands of tokens before the model has even seen your user message."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy tools fix this with "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"progressive disclosure"}]},{"type":"text","value":": mark rarely-used tools "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazy: true"},{"type":"text","value":" and they are withheld from the initial system prompt. The model sees only their names in a short \"Discoverable APIs\" catalog. When it needs one, it calls the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discover_tools"},{"type":"text","value":" sibling tool to fetch the TypeScript signature on demand, then uses it inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute_typescript"},{"type":"text","value":". All sandbox bindings are always injected — lazy only defers "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not callability."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"marking-a-tool-lazy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marking a Tool Lazy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazy: true"},{"type":"text","value":" to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":" config for any tool you want to defer:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\n// Always eager — documented upfront\nconst fetchWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"fetchWeather\",\n  description: \"Get current weather for a city\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ location: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ location }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.weather.example/v1?city=${location}`);\n  return res.json();\n});\n\n// Lazy — kept out of the system prompt until discovered\nconst fetchArchive = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"fetchArchive\",\n  description: \"Retrieve historical weather archive data for a date range\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string(),\n    from: z.string(),\n    to: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ date: z.string(), temperature: z.number() })),\n  lazy: true,\n}).server(async ({ location, from, to }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.example/v1/archive?city=${location}&from=${from}&to=${to}`\n  );\n  return res.json();\n});","lang":"typescript","meta":"group=lazy-tools"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager tools continue to receive full type stubs in the system prompt. Lazy tools appear only by name."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass both eager and lazy tools to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCodeMode"},{"type":"text","value":". When at least one tool is lazy, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCodeMode"},{"type":"text","value":" also returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discover_tools"},{"type":"text","value":" sibling tool — include it in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" array you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// server/route.ts\nimport { chat, maxIterations, toServerSentEventsStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { createCodeMode } from \"@tanstack/ai-code-mode\";\nimport { createNodeIsolateDriver } from \"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nconst { tools, systemPrompt } = createCodeMode({\n  driver: createNodeIsolateDriver(),\n  tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive], // fetchArchive is lazy\n});\n\n// tools is [execute_typescript, discover_tools]\n// — discover_tools is included automatically because fetchArchive is lazy\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    systemPrompts: [\"You are a helpful weather assistant.\", systemPrompt],\n    tools: [...tools],\n    messages,\n    agentLoopStrategy: maxIterations(10),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsStream(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript","meta":"group=lazy-tools"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCodeMode"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ tool, discoveryTool, tools, systemPrompt }"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerTool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute_typescript"},{"type":"text","value":" tool (backward compatible)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discoveryTool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerTool | null"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discover_tools"},{"type":"text","value":" tool, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" when there are no lazy tools"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Array<ServerTool>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[tool]"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[tool, discoveryTool]"},{"type":"text","value":" — spread into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ tools })"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"systemPrompt"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The matching system prompt"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If no tools are lazy, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discoveryTool"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" contains only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute_typescript"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-discover-tools-flow","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discover_tools"},{"type":"text","value":" Flow"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the model encounters a task that requires a lazy tool, it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discover_tools"},{"type":"text","value":" with the tool name (bare name, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"external_"},{"type":"text","value":" prefix)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Receives the TypeScript type stub and description for that tool."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute_typescript"},{"type":"text","value":" code using the now-documented "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"external_fetchArchive(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" call."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bindings are always injected into the sandbox — discovering a tool only retrieves documentation, it does not enable the binding. The model could call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"external_fetchArchive"},{"type":"text","value":" without discovering it first, but it would be writing blind without the type signature."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tuning-the-discoverable-apis-catalog","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tuning the Discoverable APIs Catalog"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, lazy tools appear in the system prompt as bare names with no description:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"### Discoverable APIs\n\n- external_fetchArchive\n- external_runReport\n- external_exportData","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want the model to have a hint about what each tool does before deciding whether to discover it, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazyToolsConfig.includeDescription"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCodeMode } from \"@tanstack/ai-code-mode\";\nimport { createNodeIsolateDriver } from \"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node\";\nimport {\n  fetchWeather,\n  fetchArchive,\n  runReport,\n  exportData,\n} from \"./tools\";\n\nconst { tools, systemPrompt } = createCodeMode({\n  driver: createNodeIsolateDriver(),\n  tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport, exportData],\n  lazyToolsConfig: {\n    includeDescription: \"first-sentence\", // 'none' | 'first-sentence' | 'full'\n  },\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'first-sentence'"},{"type":"text","value":" the catalog becomes:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"### Discoverable APIs\n\n- external_fetchArchive — Retrieve historical weather archive data for a date range.\n- external_runReport — Generate a summary report for a given time period.\n- external_exportData — Export query results to CSV or JSON format.","lang":"text"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Effect"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none'"},{"type":"text","value":" (default)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bare names only — smallest possible prompt addition"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'first-sentence'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Name plus the first sentence of the tool's description"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'full'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Name plus the complete description"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full type stub and input/output schema are always returned on discovery — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeDescription"},{"type":"text","value":" only affects the pre-discovery catalog."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lazy-tools-with-plain-chat","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy Tools with Plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazyToolsConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" option works for lazy tools used directly with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":", outside of Code Mode. Tools marked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazy: true"},{"type":"text","value":" are withheld from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__lazy__tool__discovery__"},{"type":"text","value":" catalog description until the model calls for them. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazyToolsConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" directly to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, maxIterations, toServerSentEventsStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport } from \"./tools\";\n\n// Non-code-mode: lazy tools in a regular chat agent\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport],\n    lazyToolsConfig: {\n      includeDescription: \"first-sentence\",\n    },\n    agentLoopStrategy: maxIterations(10),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsStream(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeDescription"},{"type":"text","value":" behavior is identical — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none'"},{"type":"text","value":" lists bare tool names, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'first-sentence'"},{"type":"text","value":" appends the first sentence, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'full'"},{"type":"text","value":" appends the complete description."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tips","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tips"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The bare-names catalog is enough for models that reason well about tool names. Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'first-sentence'"},{"type":"text","value":" only if the model frequently discovers irrelevant tools."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy tools are always callable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"external_*"},{"type":"text","value":" bindings are injected into the sandbox regardless of whether the model has called "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discover_tools"},{"type":"text","value":". Discovery only reveals documentation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discoveryTool"},{"type":"text","value":" for observability."}]},{"type":"text","value":" You can inspect "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"discoveryTool.name"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"discover_tools\""},{"type":"text","value":") to confirm the tool is wired up, or log its calls for analytics."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partition by frequency, not capability."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Mark tools lazy when they are rarely needed for a typical request. Core tools that most requests use should stay eager."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next Steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./code-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — Core Code Mode setup and API reference"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./code-mode-with-snippets","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode with Snippets"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — Persistent reusable snippet libraries"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./code-mode-isolates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolate Drivers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — Compare Node, QuickJS, and Cloudflare sandbox runtimes"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"marking-a-tool-lazy","text":"Marking a Tool Lazy","level":2},{"id":"server-setup","text":"Server Setup","level":2},{"id":"the-discover-tools-flow","text":"The discover_tools Flow","level":2},{"id":"tuning-the-discoverable-apis-catalog","text":"Tuning the Discoverable APIs Catalog","level":2},{"id":"lazy-tools-with-plain-chat","text":"Lazy Tools with Plain chat()","level":2},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next Steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nLarge tool catalogs bloat the `execute_typescript` system prompt. Every tool you pass to `createCodeMode` becomes a full TypeScript type stub in that prompt — and at 50+ tools, those stubs can push the effective prompt into the tens of thousands of tokens before the model has even seen your user message.\n\nLazy tools fix this with **progressive disclosure**: mark rarely-used tools `lazy: true` and they are withheld from the initial system prompt. The model sees only their names in a short \"Discoverable APIs\" catalog. When it needs one, it calls the `discover_tools` sibling tool to fetch the TypeScript signature on demand, then uses it inside `execute_typescript`. All sandbox bindings are always injected — lazy only defers _documentation_, not callability.\n\n## Marking a Tool Lazy\n\nAdd `lazy: true` to the `toolDefinition` config for any tool you want to defer:\n\n```typescript group=lazy-tools\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\n// Always eager — documented upfront\nconst fetchWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"fetchWeather\",\n  description: \"Get current weather for a city\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ location: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ location }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.weather.example/v1?city=${location}`);\n  return res.json();\n});\n\n// Lazy — kept out of the system prompt until discovered\nconst fetchArchive = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"fetchArchive\",\n  description: \"Retrieve historical weather archive data for a date range\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string(),\n    from: z.string(),\n    to: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ date: z.string(), temperature: z.number() })),\n  lazy: true,\n}).server(async ({ location, from, to }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.example/v1/archive?city=${location}&from=${from}&to=${to}`\n  );\n  return res.json();\n});\n```\n\nEager tools continue to receive full type stubs in the system prompt. Lazy tools appear only by name.\n\n## Server Setup\n\nPass both eager and lazy tools to `createCodeMode`. When at least one tool is lazy, `createCodeMode` also returns a `discover_tools` sibling tool — include it in the `tools` array you pass to `chat()`:\n\n```typescript group=lazy-tools\n// server/route.ts\nimport { chat, maxIterations, toServerSentEventsStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { createCodeMode } from \"@tanstack/ai-code-mode\";\nimport { createNodeIsolateDriver } from \"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nconst { tools, systemPrompt } = createCodeMode({\n  driver: createNodeIsolateDriver(),\n  tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive], // fetchArchive is lazy\n});\n\n// tools is [execute_typescript, discover_tools]\n// — discover_tools is included automatically because fetchArchive is lazy\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    systemPrompts: [\"You are a helpful weather assistant.\", systemPrompt],\n    tools: [...tools],\n    messages,\n    agentLoopStrategy: maxIterations(10),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsStream(stream);\n}\n```\n\n`createCodeMode` returns `{ tool, discoveryTool, tools, systemPrompt }`:\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| `tool` | `ServerTool` | The `execute_typescript` tool (backward compatible) |\n| `discoveryTool` | `ServerTool \\| null` | The `discover_tools` tool, or `null` when there are no lazy tools |\n| `tools` | `Array<ServerTool>` | `[tool]` or `[tool, discoveryTool]` — spread into `chat({ tools })` |\n| `systemPrompt` | `string` | The matching system prompt |\n\nIf no tools are lazy, `discoveryTool` is `null` and `tools` contains only `execute_typescript`.\n\n## The `discover_tools` Flow\n\nWhen the model encounters a task that requires a lazy tool, it:\n\n1. Calls `discover_tools` with the tool name (bare name, no `external_` prefix).\n2. Receives the TypeScript type stub and description for that tool.\n3. Writes `execute_typescript` code using the now-documented `external_fetchArchive(...)` call.\n\nThe bindings are always injected into the sandbox — discovering a tool only retrieves documentation, it does not enable the binding. The model could call `external_fetchArchive` without discovering it first, but it would be writing blind without the type signature.\n\n## Tuning the Discoverable APIs Catalog\n\nBy default, lazy tools appear in the system prompt as bare names with no description:\n\n```text\n### Discoverable APIs\n\n- external_fetchArchive\n- external_runReport\n- external_exportData\n```\n\nIf you want the model to have a hint about what each tool does before deciding whether to discover it, use `lazyToolsConfig.includeDescription`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCodeMode } from \"@tanstack/ai-code-mode\";\nimport { createNodeIsolateDriver } from \"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node\";\nimport {\n  fetchWeather,\n  fetchArchive,\n  runReport,\n  exportData,\n} from \"./tools\";\n\nconst { tools, systemPrompt } = createCodeMode({\n  driver: createNodeIsolateDriver(),\n  tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport, exportData],\n  lazyToolsConfig: {\n    includeDescription: \"first-sentence\", // 'none' | 'first-sentence' | 'full'\n  },\n});\n```\n\nWith `'first-sentence'` the catalog becomes:\n\n```text\n### Discoverable APIs\n\n- external_fetchArchive — Retrieve historical weather archive data for a date range.\n- external_runReport — Generate a summary report for a given time period.\n- external_exportData — Export query results to CSV or JSON format.\n```\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| `'none'` (default) | Bare names only — smallest possible prompt addition |\n| `'first-sentence'` | Name plus the first sentence of the tool's description |\n| `'full'` | Name plus the complete description |\n\nThe full type stub and input/output schema are always returned on discovery — `includeDescription` only affects the pre-discovery catalog.\n\n## Lazy Tools with Plain `chat()`\n\nThe same `lazyToolsConfig` option works for lazy tools used directly with `chat()`, outside of Code Mode. Tools marked `lazy: true` are withheld from the `__lazy__tool__discovery__` catalog description until the model calls for them. Pass `lazyToolsConfig` directly to `chat()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, maxIterations, toServerSentEventsStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport } from \"./tools\";\n\n// Non-code-mode: lazy tools in a regular chat agent\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport],\n    lazyToolsConfig: {\n      includeDescription: \"first-sentence\",\n    },\n    agentLoopStrategy: maxIterations(10),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsStream(stream);\n}\n```\n\nThe `includeDescription` behavior is identical — `'none'` lists bare tool names, `'first-sentence'` appends the first sentence, `'full'` appends the complete description.\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Start with `'none'`.** The bare-names catalog is enough for models that reason well about tool names. Add `'first-sentence'` only if the model frequently discovers irrelevant tools.\n- **Lazy tools are always callable.** Their `external_*` bindings are injected into the sandbox regardless of whether the model has called `discover_tools`. Discovery only reveals documentation.\n- **Use `discoveryTool` for observability.** You can inspect `discoveryTool.name` (`\"discover_tools\"`) to confirm the tool is wired up, or log its calls for analytics.\n- **Partition by frequency, not capability.** Mark tools lazy when they are rarely needed for a typical request. Core tools that most requests use should stay eager.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [Code Mode](./code-mode) — Core Code Mode setup and API reference\n- [Code Mode with Snippets](./code-mode-with-snippets) — Persistent reusable snippet libraries\n- [Isolate Drivers](./code-mode-isolates) — Compare Node, QuickJS, and Cloudflare sandbox runtimes\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Lazy Tools\nid: lazy-tools\norder: 5\ndescription: \"Keep large tool catalogs out of the Code Mode system prompt with lazy tools — the model fetches TypeScript signatures on demand via a discover_tools call.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - code mode\n  - lazy tools\n  - discover_tools\n  - progressive disclosure\n  - prompt size\n  - tool catalog\n---\n\nLarge tool catalogs bloat the `execute_typescript` system prompt. Every tool you pass to `createCodeMode` becomes a full TypeScript type stub in that prompt — and at 50+ tools, those stubs can push the effective prompt into the tens of thousands of tokens before the model has even seen your user message.\n\nLazy tools fix this with **progressive disclosure**: mark rarely-used tools `lazy: true` and they are withheld from the initial system prompt. The model sees only their names in a short \"Discoverable APIs\" catalog. When it needs one, it calls the `discover_tools` sibling tool to fetch the TypeScript signature on demand, then uses it inside `execute_typescript`. All sandbox bindings are always injected — lazy only defers _documentation_, not callability.\n\n## Marking a Tool Lazy\n\nAdd `lazy: true` to the `toolDefinition` config for any tool you want to defer:\n\n```typescript group=lazy-tools\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\n// Always eager — documented upfront\nconst fetchWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"fetchWeather\",\n  description: \"Get current weather for a city\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ location: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temperature: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ location }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.weather.example/v1?city=${location}`);\n  return res.json();\n});\n\n// Lazy — kept out of the system prompt until discovered\nconst fetchArchive = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"fetchArchive\",\n  description: \"Retrieve historical weather archive data for a date range\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string(),\n    from: z.string(),\n    to: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ date: z.string(), temperature: z.number() })),\n  lazy: true,\n}).server(async ({ location, from, to }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(\n    `https://api.weather.example/v1/archive?city=${location}&from=${from}&to=${to}`\n  );\n  return res.json();\n});\n```\n\nEager tools continue to receive full type stubs in the system prompt. Lazy tools appear only by name.\n\n## Server Setup\n\nPass both eager and lazy tools to `createCodeMode`. When at least one tool is lazy, `createCodeMode` also returns a `discover_tools` sibling tool — include it in the `tools` array you pass to `chat()`:\n\n```typescript group=lazy-tools\n// server/route.ts\nimport { chat, maxIterations, toServerSentEventsStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { createCodeMode } from \"@tanstack/ai-code-mode\";\nimport { createNodeIsolateDriver } from \"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nconst { tools, systemPrompt } = createCodeMode({\n  driver: createNodeIsolateDriver(),\n  tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive], // fetchArchive is lazy\n});\n\n// tools is [execute_typescript, discover_tools]\n// — discover_tools is included automatically because fetchArchive is lazy\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    systemPrompts: [\"You are a helpful weather assistant.\", systemPrompt],\n    tools: [...tools],\n    messages,\n    agentLoopStrategy: maxIterations(10),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsStream(stream);\n}\n```\n\n`createCodeMode` returns `{ tool, discoveryTool, tools, systemPrompt }`:\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| `tool` | `ServerTool` | The `execute_typescript` tool (backward compatible) |\n| `discoveryTool` | `ServerTool \\| null` | The `discover_tools` tool, or `null` when there are no lazy tools |\n| `tools` | `Array<ServerTool>` | `[tool]` or `[tool, discoveryTool]` — spread into `chat({ tools })` |\n| `systemPrompt` | `string` | The matching system prompt |\n\nIf no tools are lazy, `discoveryTool` is `null` and `tools` contains only `execute_typescript`.\n\n## The `discover_tools` Flow\n\nWhen the model encounters a task that requires a lazy tool, it:\n\n1. Calls `discover_tools` with the tool name (bare name, no `external_` prefix).\n2. Receives the TypeScript type stub and description for that tool.\n3. Writes `execute_typescript` code using the now-documented `external_fetchArchive(...)` call.\n\nThe bindings are always injected into the sandbox — discovering a tool only retrieves documentation, it does not enable the binding. The model could call `external_fetchArchive` without discovering it first, but it would be writing blind without the type signature.\n\n## Tuning the Discoverable APIs Catalog\n\nBy default, lazy tools appear in the system prompt as bare names with no description:\n\n```text\n### Discoverable APIs\n\n- external_fetchArchive\n- external_runReport\n- external_exportData\n```\n\nIf you want the model to have a hint about what each tool does before deciding whether to discover it, use `lazyToolsConfig.includeDescription`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { createCodeMode } from \"@tanstack/ai-code-mode\";\nimport { createNodeIsolateDriver } from \"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node\";\nimport {\n  fetchWeather,\n  fetchArchive,\n  runReport,\n  exportData,\n} from \"./tools\";\n\nconst { tools, systemPrompt } = createCodeMode({\n  driver: createNodeIsolateDriver(),\n  tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport, exportData],\n  lazyToolsConfig: {\n    includeDescription: \"first-sentence\", // 'none' | 'first-sentence' | 'full'\n  },\n});\n```\n\nWith `'first-sentence'` the catalog becomes:\n\n```text\n### Discoverable APIs\n\n- external_fetchArchive — Retrieve historical weather archive data for a date range.\n- external_runReport — Generate a summary report for a given time period.\n- external_exportData — Export query results to CSV or JSON format.\n```\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| `'none'` (default) | Bare names only — smallest possible prompt addition |\n| `'first-sentence'` | Name plus the first sentence of the tool's description |\n| `'full'` | Name plus the complete description |\n\nThe full type stub and input/output schema are always returned on discovery — `includeDescription` only affects the pre-discovery catalog.\n\n## Lazy Tools with Plain `chat()`\n\nThe same `lazyToolsConfig` option works for lazy tools used directly with `chat()`, outside of Code Mode. Tools marked `lazy: true` are withheld from the `__lazy__tool__discovery__` catalog description until the model calls for them. Pass `lazyToolsConfig` directly to `chat()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, maxIterations, toServerSentEventsStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport } from \"./tools\";\n\n// Non-code-mode: lazy tools in a regular chat agent\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await req.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [fetchWeather, fetchArchive, runReport],\n    lazyToolsConfig: {\n      includeDescription: \"first-sentence\",\n    },\n    agentLoopStrategy: maxIterations(10),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsStream(stream);\n}\n```\n\nThe `includeDescription` behavior is identical — `'none'` lists bare tool names, `'first-sentence'` appends the first sentence, `'full'` appends the complete description.\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Start with `'none'`.** The bare-names catalog is enough for models that reason well about tool names. Add `'first-sentence'` only if the model frequently discovers irrelevant tools.\n- **Lazy tools are always callable.** Their `external_*` bindings are injected into the sandbox regardless of whether the model has called `discover_tools`. Discovery only reveals documentation.\n- **Use `discoveryTool` for observability.** You can inspect `discoveryTool.name` (`\"discover_tools\"`) to confirm the tool is wired up, or log its calls for analytics.\n- **Partition by frequency, not capability.** Mark tools lazy when they are rarely needed for a typical request. Core tools that most requests use should stay eager.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [Code Mode](./code-mode) — Core Code Mode setup and API reference\n- [Code Mode with Snippets](./code-mode-with-snippets) — Persistent reusable snippet libraries\n- [Isolate Drivers](./code-mode-isolates) — Compare Node, QuickJS, and Cloudflare sandbox runtimes\n"}
